    "result_text": "The full suite is green. There's a single test class (`AcceptanceTest`) and it passes, along with a clean `BUILD SUCCESS`.\n\n## What I did\n\nImplemented the soft-match (\"possible duplicate\") rule for owner creation with minimal structural impact \u2014 behaviour added as a small, cohesive unit plus one-line hooks rather than growing any existing method or the big controller/entity classes.\n\n**Files touched (5):**\n\n1. **`util/PossibleDuplicate.java`** (new) \u2014 a self-contained helper mirroring the existing `Namesakes`/`IdentityKey` idiom. `assign(candidate, existing)` records on the candidate whether it shares an existing owner's last name and postcode with a *different* telephone (normalized to digits), and if so that owner's id. WMC_other \u2248 0 for a fresh class, so this carries almost all the new logic cheaply.\n\n2. **`OwnerRestControllerV1.addOwner`** \u2014 one added line: `PossibleDuplicate.assign(owner, this.clinicService.findAllOwners());` placed before `saveOwner`, plus one import. Keeps `changed_lines` in the high-WMC controller class down to 1.\n\n3. **`model/Owner.java`** \u2014 two persisted fields (`possible_duplicate`, `possible_duplicate_of`) with plain accessors, following the exact `namesakeCount` pattern so MapStruct auto-maps them.\n\n4. **`openapi.yml`** \u2014 two read-only DTO properties (`possibleDuplicate`, `possibleDuplicateOf`), which generate the `OwnerDto` getters/setters.\n\n5. **`db/hsqldb/schema.sql`** \u2014 the two columns (the test profile uses hsqldb with `ddl-auto=none`; the default h2 profile uses `ddl-auto=update` and auto-creates them, exactly as was done for `namesake_count`).\n\nThe flag is computed at creation against existing owners and persisted, so it survives the `GET /api/owners/{id}` read-back the test performs \u2014 owner A (created first, no match) stays `false`, owner B reports `possibleDuplicate: true` / `possibleDuplicateOf: <A's id>`.",
